WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:07.154
An exact system asks for an input that satisfies every observation simultaneously. If two equations demand

00:00:07.154 --> 00:00:14.108
different values for the same expression, no input can satisfy both. This is inconsistency, even if

00:00:14.108 --> 00:00:17.150
the coefficient matrix has a familiar shape.

00:00:17.667 --> 00:00:22.697
Both equations ask about the same sum. They cannot determine x and y individually.

00:00:23.200 --> 00:00:30.930
Consistency asks whether all equations can hold simultaneously. Counting equations alone does not answer that question.

00:00:31.433 --> 00:00:37.576
When the observations agree, do they determine both unknowns uniquely? Pause here and make a prediction

00:00:37.576 --> 00:00:38.343
before continuing.

00:00:39.467 --> 00:00:46.196
The least-squares compromise is the mean of the two requested sums. This changes the objective from

00:00:46.196 --> 00:00:52.925
exact matching to minimum squared error. For our starting inputs, First target: 1. Second target: 2.

00:00:52.925 --> 00:00:54.187
Fitted sum: 1.5.

00:00:54.700 --> 00:01:00.731
The residual entries sum to zero. Even after fitting, x and y remain underdetermined: any pair

00:01:00.731 --> 00:01:03.370
with this fitted sum is a minimizer.

00:01:03.900 --> 00:01:09.760
Check consistency, identifiable quantities and uniqueness separately before choosing a solver.

